## Why put the Executive Summary first?

This follows professional consultancy report practice. Executives typically read the summary before deciding whether to read the technical report. The marking guide also states:

> "The executive summary is a standalone document."

That means it should:

* stand on its own,
* avoid technical jargon,
* summarise the key findings and recommendations,
* be understandable without reading the rest of the report.

---

## What each section should contain

### Executive Summary (400–600 words)

Audience: Board of Directors

Include:

* Current security risks in plain English.
* Why these risks matter to the business.
* The three highest-priority recommendations.
* POPIA implications.
* Estimated cost/implementation effort (high level).
* Business benefits.

Avoid:

* Firewall ports.
* IDS/IPS terminology without explanation.
* Technical configurations.

---

### Introduction

Audience: Technical reader (IT Officer and assessor)

Include:

* Purpose.
* Scope.
* Assumptions.
* Methodology.

Approximately 1 page.

